# bench/labels/leaky-repo.truth - the HAND-AUTHORED ground truth for the
# leaky-repo secrets corpus, pinned at bench/corpus.lock's `leaky-repo` commit.
#
# WHY THIS CORPUS AND NOT SecretBench.  SecretBench (setu1421/SecretBench,
# MSR'23) is the best-labelled public secrets dataset and is NOT OBTAINABLE
# HERE: the repository of that name holds only metadata, and the 97,479
# labelled secrets live in Google BigQuery and Google Cloud Storage behind a
# Google Cloud account, a signed data-protection agreement with the authors,
# and per-email access they grant by hand.  That is recorded as an explicit
# not-measured cell in the leg README rather than quietly substituted.
#
# leaky-repo is the substitute: MIT, third-party, unchanged since 2020, and
# deliberately seeded with the credential-bearing files that most often reach a
# public repository by accident.  Its author states plainly that none of the
# values are real - they are randomised or redacted reproductions of shapes
# seen in the wild.
#
# ITS RELATIONSHIP TO THE TOOLS BEING SCORED, stated rather than left to be
# found.  It is not any scanned tool's own fixture set, which is what
# methodology rule R1 requires.  It does ship `.leaky-meta/benchmarking/*.md`
# reporting how Gitleaks, TruffleHog, gitrob and detect-secrets scored against
# it, so a tool author COULD have tuned against it at some point since 2019.
# That is a weaker relationship than authorship, and it cuts against scoursh
# rather than for it, so it is disclosed here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE UNIT IS ONE PLANTED CREDENTIAL, at its own line or line range
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A `real: true` case is one credential; a file holding three distinct
# credentials is three cases, because finding one of them is not finding the
# other two.  A multi-line secret (a PEM key, a WordPress salt block, a SQL
# credential dump) is one case spanning its real extent, so a tool anchoring
# anywhere inside it is credited once.
#
# A `real: false` case is a NEGATIVE CONTROL: a contiguous region carrying no
# credential at all, chosen where a detector plausibly errs - a public key
# beside a private one, an entropy-dense string that authenticates nothing, the
# word `password` as a column name or a class path, a config block of hosts and
# usernames.  A tool that reports a finding inside one of these is counted as a
# false positive, and that is the whole reason they are here: recall alone is a
# metric that a rule flagging every line scores 100% on.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY LEFT UNLABELLED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lines that are genuinely ambiguous carry NO case, in either direction, and
# are therefore neither credited nor penalised.  The clearest example is an AWS
# access key ID with no secret beside it: leaky-repo's own secrets.csv calls it
# `informative`, a reasonable scanner reports it, and calling that a false
# positive would be a judgement this label set should not make silently.  The
# same applies to the two binary Firefox stores `cert9.db` and `key4.db`, and
# to `.leaky-meta/`, which is the corpus's notes about itself.
#
# Leaving a region unlabelled is not free and is not hidden: the leg README
# reports, per tool, how many of its findings landed outside every labelled
# range, so a reader can see how much of each tool's output this label set
# declined to judge.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CROSS-CHECK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# leaky-repo ships `.leaky-meta/secrets.csv`, a per-FILE count of `risk` and
# `informative` lines covering part of the tree.  It is NOT the labels - it has
# no line numbers, it omits many of the files below, and a benchmark whose
# truth is a count cannot score a line - but it is an independent second
# opinion, and where a call below agrees or disagrees with it the rationale
# says so.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT: bench/lib/truth.sh records - five 0x1f-separated fields plus a sixth.
#
#     <case><US><file><US><category><US><cwe><US><real><US><line-range>
#
# The sixth field is the credential's line range and is what `bench/score.sh
# --match line` scores against.  `cwe` is EMPTY for every case here, and that
# is a statement rather than an omission: every planted credential in this
# corpus is CWE-798, so a strict-CWE column would be a tautology - it would
# separate no two tools and no two cases.  The scorer renders the strict column
# for such a category as an explicit `no CWE in truth` cell rather than as a
# row of zeros.
#
# 82 cases: 65 planted secrets, 17 negative controls.

# ==========================================================================
# .bash_profile
# ==========================================================================

# aws-secret-access-key  (line 12)
#   An AWS secret access key assigned to AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY. The secret
#   half of a credential pair, in a file every login shell sources.
.bash_profile:aws-secret-access-key.bash_profilesecretstrue12

# rds-password  (line 20)
#   RDS_PASSWORD as a literal. The RDS hostname two lines earlier makes it
#   usable.
.bash_profile:rds-password.bash_profilesecretstrue20

# heroku-api-key  (line 21)
#   HEROKU_API_KEY as a literal. A Heroku API key is a full-account
#   credential.
.bash_profile:heroku-api-key.bash_profilesecretstrue21

# github-api-token  (line 22)
#   A 40-hex GitHub token in HOMEBREW_GITHUB_API_TOKEN - the classic
#   personal access token shape.
.bash_profile:github-api-token.bash_profilesecretstrue22

# slack-api-token  (line 23)
#   An `xoxp-` Slack user token. Provider-prefixed and unambiguous.
.bash_profile:slack-api-token.bash_profilesecretstrue23

# mlab-password  (line 24)
#   MLAB_PASS as a literal, with the database host and name beside it.
.bash_profile:mlab-password.bash_profilesecretstrue24

# informative-not-secret  (lines 13-19)
#   NEGATIVE CONTROL. A region, an AWS region alias, a bucket name and an
#   RDS hostname - identifiers that disclose infrastructure but authenticate
#   nothing. leaky-repo's own secrets.csv classes each of these
#   `informative` rather than `risk`, which is an independent second opinion
#   on the call.
.bash_profile:informative-not-secret.bash_profilesecretsfalse13-19

# ==========================================================================
# .bashrc
# ==========================================================================

# gmail-password  (line 105)
#   GMAIL_PASSWORD as a literal, with the account name on the line above.
.bashrc:gmail-password.bashrcsecretstrue105

# mailchimp-api-key  (line 106)
#   A Mailchimp API key in its documented `<32 hex>-<dc>` form.
.bashrc:mailchimp-api-key.bashrcsecretstrue106

# jekyll-github-token  (line 109)
#   A 40-hex GitHub token in JEKYLL_GITHUB_TOKEN.
.bashrc:jekyll-github-token.bashrcsecretstrue109

# shell-config-not-secret  (lines 1-100)
#   NEGATIVE CONTROL. A hundred lines of stock Debian .bashrc - history
#   settings, prompt escapes, ls aliases and an `alert` alias containing a
#   sed program. Nothing here authenticates anything, and the escape-heavy
#   prompt strings are exactly the shape a naive entropy detector mistakes
#   for a key.
.bashrc:shell-config-not-secret.bashrcsecretsfalse1-100

